// =============================================================================
// 11-search-by-http-result-code.kql
// Buckets Search-side outcomes so 206, 403, 429, and 503 are separable.
// =============================================================================
//
// RUN THE DISCOVERY QUERIES FIRST
//   00-discover-dependency-types.kql and 01-discover-search-diagnostic-shape.kql.
//   Section 3 of file 01 decides whether the 206 bucket below can ever populate.
//   If your workspace reports different column names, substitute them here.
//
// PURPOSE
//   Split every query-side Search operation by HTTP status over time, keeping
//   the four failure modes distinct because they mean four different things:
//     206  semantic ranker at capacity, partial results returned
//     403  authorization or network policy denial, NOT quota and NOT storage
//     429  request throttled
//     503  service throttled or unavailable
//
// WHY THE DISTINCTION MATTERS
//   Azure AI Search never returns 403 for quota or for storage exhaustion.
//   Semantic free-plan exhaustion returns 402. So a 403 here is an auth, RBAC,
//   or firewall problem and belongs to a completely separate workstream from
//   the 206s. Bucketing them together hides that.
//   Zero 429s is the expected result for this workload rather than evidence
//   against resource constraint: the semantic ranker signals capacity pressure
//   with 206, not with 429.
//   Research: .copilot-tracking/research/2026-09-22/ai-search-scaling-206-403-rca-research.md
//             Lines 126-137 (D4, why no 429s), Lines 142-163 (D6, what 403 means)
//
// TARGET TABLE
//   AzureDiagnostics, ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.SEARCH".
//
// PREREQUISITES
//   * Search diagnostic setting sending OperationLogs to this workspace.
//   Enable with: scripts/Enable-DiagnosticSettings.ps1
//
// UNVERIFIED ASSUMPTION (open item U6)
//   No Microsoft sample confirms that Search writes 206 into resultSignature_d.
//   The 206 bucket below is therefore UNVERIFIED. If file 01 section 3 returns
//   no 206 rows, this query cannot detect semantic partial responses at all and
//   21-apim-206-semantic-partial-capture.kql is the only route to them.
//
// HEALTHY VS UNHEALTHY
//   Healthy   : effectively all traffic in the 200 bucket.
//   Unhealthy : any sustained 403 band (auth or network policy is breaking),
//               any 206 band (semantic ranker is saturating), or 429 and 503
//               bands (the service is throttling outright).
//   Escalate  : 206 sustained above roughly 1 percent of query volume. That
//               threshold is an engineering recommendation, not a Microsoft
//               published figure. Tune it against two weeks of baseline.
//
// Derived from Q2-SearchByHttpResultCode,
//   .copilot-tracking/research/2026-09-22/ai-search-scaling-206-403-rca-research.md Lines 293-310
// =============================================================================

let lookback = 24h;
let bucket = 5m;
AzureDiagnostics
| where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
| where ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.SEARCH"
| where OperationName in ("Query.Search", "Query.Suggest", "Query.Lookup", "Query.Autocomplete")
| extend StatusCode = toint(resultSignature_d)
| extend StatusBucket = case(
      StatusCode == 200, "200 OK",
      StatusCode == 206, "206 Partial Content (semantic capacity)",   // UNVERIFIED that Search logs 206 - see U6
      StatusCode == 402, "402 Payment Required (semantic free plan exhausted)",
      StatusCode == 403, "403 Forbidden (auth / network policy)",
      StatusCode == 429, "429 Too Many Requests (throttle)",
      StatusCode == 503, "503 Service Unavailable (throttle)",
      strcat(tostring(StatusCode), " Other"))
| summarize Requests = count() by StatusBucket, bin(TimeGenerated, bucket)
| render timechart with (ytitle = "Search requests by status")

